---
import DocsPage from '../../components/DocsPage.astro'
import { sourceUrl } from '../../data/source-projection'
import { sitePath } from '../../data/site-path'
---

<DocsPage
  title="React workbook product"
  eyebrow="PRIVATE VITE PRODUCT"
  summary="Run the repository's complete React workbook against its Rust/WASM worker."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/docs/react/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-warning" aria-label="React product status">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">STATUS: REPOSITORY PRODUCT</p>
      <p>
        <code>excel/react-excel</code> is a private Vite application, not an installable integration library.
        Run it from this repository and treat its source as one product rather than a host-facing API.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>1. Run the workbook</h2>
    <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/allroad88888888/einfach-excel.git
cd einfach-excel
pnpm install
pnpm --filter @einfach/react-excel dev`}</code></pre>
    <p>The development server listens on <code>http://127.0.0.1:5183</code>.</p>

    <h2>2. Follow the Rust-only runtime</h2>
    <p>
      The application creates its backend from <code>@einfach/solid-excel/vnext-worker-runtime?worker</code>.
      Workbook reads and acknowledged edits cross the worker boundary into Rust/WASM; there is no TypeScript engine,
      static backend, or runtime fallback.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Exercise the current product</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-terms">
      <article><strong>Bounded projection</strong><span>Browse 1,000 sales orders while React mounts a 32-row window.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Selection</strong><span>Select one cell or drag a range, including after scrolling to the final record.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Cell editing</strong><span>Double-click or press Enter, then commit through Rust with Enter or blur.</span></article>
    </div>
    <p>Press Escape to cancel an edit without writing it.</p>

    <h2>4. Verify the product</h2>
    <pre><code>{`pnpm --filter @einfach/react-excel typecheck
pnpm --filter @einfach/react-excel build
pnpm --filter @einfach/react-excel test`}</code></pre>

    <h2>5. Read the product source</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/react-excel/README.md')}><strong>Read the product boundary</strong><span>See current abilities, directories, and commands. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/react-excel/src/app/App.tsx')}><strong>Read application startup</strong><span>Follow Rust backend readiness into the workbook runtime. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/react-excel/src/workbook/backend/rust-backend.ts')}><strong>Read the worker boundary</strong><span>See seed import and Rust/WASM backend creation. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>Current boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The ribbon, formula bar, sheet tab, and zoom surfaces are presentational. Clipboard, history, formatting,
      sheet commands, and other workbook commands are not wired, and this private product makes no npm publication
      or browser-support promise.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
